<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bretton Woods system</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bretton Woods system was the international monetary order agreed at the Bretton Woods Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> in July 1944. It fixed exchange rates against the US dollar, which was convertible into gold at 35 dollars an ounce, and created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> and the World Bank; it broke down after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ended gold convertibility in 1971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bretton-woods-system.jpg" alt="Bretton Woods system" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sven Klippel · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Washington_Hotel_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The planners at Bretton Woods hoped to avoid the economic tension that followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. Creditors' intransigence over Allied war debts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, together with isolationism, had broken down the international financial system and led to a worldwide depression. The architects of the new order took as their principles no more beggar thy neighbour and control of speculative capital flows.</p><p>Delegates from 44 Allied countries met at the Mount Washington Hotel in Bretton Woods, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a>, from 1 to 22 July 1944 and signed the agreement on the last day. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-maynard-keynes">John Maynard Keynes</a> proposed that surplus countries be forced to import from or donate to debtor states, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> opposed this. The United States, which controlled two-thirds of the world's gold, insisted that the system rest on both gold and the dollar. Soviet representatives attended but declined to ratify the agreements.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The rules provided for fixed exchange rates and committed members to convertibility and free trade. The institutions became operational in 1945. Western European currencies returned to convertibility at the end of 1958 and the yen in 1964. The Eurodollar market grew from nothing before 1957 to $80 billion in 1972, and capital flows came to dwarf efforts to regulate them. By the mid-1960s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> had become economic powers in their own right, and the dollar, burdened by the cost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, was increasingly overvalued. The London Gold Pool collapsed in March 1968.</p><p>On 15 August 1971 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> ended the convertibility of the dollar to gold. In December 1971 the Group of Ten signed the Smithsonian Agreement, which set the dollar at $38 an ounce, but it did not restore the system. Many fixed currencies, such as the pound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a>, soon floated, and the end of Bretton Woods was formally ratified by the Jamaica Accords in 1976.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">IMF</a> and the World Bank, created by the conference, outlasted the system. Critics argue that reliance on the dollar let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> run deficits with fewer constraints than other nations and that fixed rates limited the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. Barry Eichengreen attributes its success to low international capital mobility, tight financial regulation and the dominant position of the United States and the dollar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bretton Woods system” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>As a permanent member of the United Nations Security Council, a founder of NATO and a nuclear power, it shaped the post-war order, and John Maynard Keynes helped design the <b>Bretton Woods system</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
